import json

with open('complete-qa.json') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

def write_empty(idx, question, direct_answer, intent, z3_secs, sp, kp):
    q = data['questions'][idx]
    q['question'] = question
    q['direct_answer'] = direct_answer
    q['interviewer_intent'] = intent
    q['answer']['sections'] = z3_secs + [
        {"type":"speakable_answer","title":"How to Answer This Verbally","content":sp},
        {"type":"key_points","title":"Key Points","content":kp}
    ]

# Q22: Multiple DataSources
write_empty(22,
    "How do you configure multiple DataSources in a Spring Boot application with routing?",
    "Spring Boot auto-configures a single DataSource. For multiple databases, manually define each DataSource as a @Bean, create separate EntityManagerFactory and TransactionManager per DataSource, and use @Qualifier to inject the correct one. AbstractRoutingDataSource dynamically routes queries to different databases based on a context key (e.g., tenant ID or read/write split).",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate can configure multiple databases and understands transaction manager isolation.","common_mistake":"Forgetting that each DataSource needs its own EntityManagerFactory and TransactionManager \u2014 mixing them causes entities to be managed by the wrong persistence context.","to_stand_out":"Explain AbstractRoutingDataSource for read/write splitting or multi-tenancy, and discuss how @Transactional must specify which transaction manager to use."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"One DataSource Is Auto-Configured \u2014 Two Requires Manual Wiring of Everything",
         "content":"Spring Boot's auto-configuration assumes a single database. Add `spring.datasource.url` and everything works. **The moment you need a second database**, auto-configuration can't decide which DataSource to use for which entities, so you must configure everything manually: two `DataSource` beans, two `LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean` instances (each scanning different entity packages), and two `PlatformTransactionManager` beans.\n\nThe critical mistake is sharing a single `EntityManagerFactory` across both databases. Each `EntityManagerFactory` must know which entities it manages and which `DataSource` to use. If they overlap, entities end up in the wrong persistence context, and queries hit the wrong database.\n\n**`AbstractRoutingDataSource`** is the alternative for dynamic routing \u2014 a single logical DataSource that delegates to different physical databases based on a context key. Use it for **read/write splitting** (route reads to replicas, writes to primary) or **multi-tenancy** (route each tenant to their database). The context key is typically stored in a `ThreadLocal` set by an interceptor or filter."},
        {"type":"step","title":"AbstractRoutingDataSource for Read/Write Splitting \u2014 The Production Pattern",
         "content":"The routing DataSource determines the target at query time by calling `determineCurrentLookupKey()`. A `ThreadLocal<String>` holds the context \u2014 set to \"primary\" for writes and \"replica\" for reads.\n\n**`@Transactional(readOnly = true)` is the natural split signal.** A custom `AbstractRoutingDataSource` checks the current transaction's read-only flag and routes accordingly. Read-only transactions go to the replica; write transactions go to the primary.\n\nThe timing subtlety: **the DataSource is selected when the connection is acquired** \u2014 at the start of the transaction. If you set the routing key inside the transaction, it's too late. The routing key must be set before `@Transactional` begins, typically in an AOP aspect that runs before the transaction advice.\n\n**Each transaction manager must specify which DataSource it uses.** With `@Transactional(\"primaryTxManager\")`, you explicitly name the manager. Without the qualifier, Spring uses the `@Primary` transaction manager, which can silently route operations to the wrong database."},
    ],
    "Spring Boot auto-configures a single DataSource. The moment you need two databases, you must manually create everything \u2014 two DataSource beans, two EntityManagerFactory instances scanning different entity packages, and two TransactionManager beans.\n\nThe critical mistake is sharing an EntityManagerFactory across databases \u2014 entities end up in the wrong persistence context.\n\n**`AbstractRoutingDataSource`** handles dynamic routing with a single logical DataSource. For read/write splitting, check `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` and route reads to replicas, writes to primary.\n\nThe timing is critical: the DataSource is selected when the connection is acquired at transaction start. The routing key must be set **before** `@Transactional` begins, not inside it.\n\nEach `@Transactional` must specify which transaction manager with a qualifier. Without it, Spring uses `@Primary` and may silently route to the wrong database.\n\nIn practice, I use `AbstractRoutingDataSource` for read/write splitting and separate EntityManagerFactories only when entities belong to genuinely different databases.",
    "- **Each DataSource needs its own EntityManagerFactory and TransactionManager** \u2014 sharing them causes entities to be managed by the wrong persistence context; use `@Qualifier` to inject the correct one.\n- **AbstractRoutingDataSource dynamically selects the target database** \u2014 `determineCurrentLookupKey()` reads a ThreadLocal context key; use for read/write splitting or multi-tenancy routing.\n- **DataSource is selected at connection acquisition, before the transaction body** \u2014 setting the routing key inside `@Transactional` is too late; use an AOP aspect that runs before transaction advice.\n- **@Transactional must specify the transaction manager qualifier** \u2014 without `@Transactional(\"primaryTxManager\")`, Spring uses `@Primary` which may silently route to the wrong database.\n- **readOnly=true is the natural signal for read/write splitting** \u2014 route `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` to replicas; this leverages existing annotations without adding new routing logic to service code."
)

# Q23: JPA Entity Relationships
write_empty(23,
    "How do you map JPA entity relationships \u2014 @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, @ManyToMany?",
    "@ManyToOne is the owning side that maps to the foreign key column. @OneToMany with mappedBy is the inverse side that mirrors the relationship. @ManyToMany creates a join table. The owning side determines which entity's changes are persisted to the database. Bidirectional relationships require synchronizing both sides in Java code.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate understands owning vs inverse side and can map relationships correctly.","common_mistake":"Not setting mappedBy on @OneToMany, causing Hibernate to create an extra join table instead of using the foreign key on the @ManyToOne side.","to_stand_out":"Explain why bidirectional @ManyToMany should use a Set (not List) to avoid duplicate rows, and mention the extra join table anti-pattern when mappedBy is missing."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"Missing mappedBy Creates an Extra Join Table \u2014 The Most Common Mapping Mistake",
         "content":"The most common JPA relationship bug is a `@OneToMany` without `mappedBy`. Hibernate interprets this as a **unidirectional one-to-many**, which it implements with a join table \u2014 an extra `order_items` table with `order_id` and `item_id` columns. Adding `mappedBy = \"order\"` tells Hibernate that the `@ManyToOne` on `Item` is the owning side, and the foreign key `order_id` lives directly on the `items` table. No join table needed.\n\nThe **owning side** is the side that controls the foreign key column in the database. For `@ManyToOne` / `@OneToMany`, the owning side is always `@ManyToOne` \u2014 it maps to the actual foreign key column. The `@OneToMany` with `mappedBy` is the **inverse side** \u2014 it reads the relationship but doesn't write it.\n\nThis distinction means that **adding an item to `order.getItems()` without setting `item.setOrder(order)` doesn't persist the relationship.** Only changes on the owning side are written to the database. Bidirectional relationships require synchronizing both sides in Java code."},
        {"type":"step","title":"@ManyToMany with Set \u2014 And Why List Causes Duplicate Rows",
         "content":"`@ManyToMany` creates a join table automatically. A `Student` with many `Course` entities and vice versa produces a `student_courses` table. The owning side (whichever doesn't have `mappedBy`) controls the join table.\n\n**Use `Set<Course>` instead of `List<Course>`** for `@ManyToMany` collections. With `List`, Hibernate handles element removal by deleting all rows in the join table and re-inserting the remaining ones. With `Set`, Hibernate removes only the specific join table row. On a student with 50 courses, removing one course with `List` fires 50 DELETEs + 49 INSERTs instead of 1 DELETE.\n\nFor bidirectional relationships, **always provide helper methods** that synchronize both sides. A `student.addCourse(course)` method should add the course to the student's set and add the student to the course's set. Without this, the in-memory state diverges from the database state within the same transaction."},
    ],
    "The key concept is **owning side vs inverse side.** The owning side controls the foreign key in the database. For `@ManyToOne` / `@OneToMany`, the owning side is always `@ManyToOne`. The `@OneToMany` with `mappedBy` is the inverse \u2014 it reads but doesn't write.\n\nThe most common mistake is `@OneToMany` without `mappedBy` \u2014 Hibernate creates an unnecessary join table instead of using the foreign key column on the child table.\n\nOnly changes on the owning side persist. Adding an item to `order.getItems()` without setting `item.setOrder(order)` doesn't save the relationship.\n\nFor `@ManyToMany`, use `Set` instead of `List`. With `List`, removing one element deletes all join table rows and re-inserts the rest. With `Set`, only the specific row is removed.\n\nIn practice, always provide helper methods that synchronize both sides of bidirectional relationships. It's the only way to keep in-memory state consistent with the database.",
    "- **Missing mappedBy on @OneToMany creates an unnecessary join table** \u2014 Hibernate treats it as unidirectional and creates an extra table; add `mappedBy = \"parentField\"` to use the existing foreign key column.\n- **Only owning side changes are persisted** \u2014 adding to the inverse collection without setting the owning side's reference silently doesn't save; synchronize both sides in helper methods.\n- **@ManyToMany with List causes bulk delete + re-insert on remove** \u2014 removing one element from a List deletes all join table rows and re-inserts the rest; use Set for single-row removal.\n- **@ManyToOne is always the owning side** \u2014 it maps to the actual foreign key column in the database; `@OneToMany` with mappedBy is the inverse side that mirrors the relationship.\n- **Bidirectional helper methods prevent state divergence** \u2014 `parent.addChild(child)` should set both `parent.getChildren().add(child)` and `child.setParent(parent)` to keep in-memory and database state consistent."
)

# Q24: Cascade Types and orphanRemoval
write_empty(24,
    "What are JPA cascade types and orphanRemoval, and when should you use each?",
    "Cascade types propagate entity operations (persist, merge, remove) from parent to child entities. CascadeType.ALL propagates everything. orphanRemoval=true automatically deletes child entities when they're removed from the parent's collection. The key difference: cascade REMOVE deletes children when the parent is deleted; orphanRemoval deletes children when they're removed from the collection, even if the parent still exists.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate understands the difference between cascade REMOVE and orphanRemoval, and when each is appropriate.","common_mistake":"Using CascadeType.ALL on @ManyToMany \u2014 deleting one student cascades to delete courses, which cascades to delete other students enrolled in those courses.","to_stand_out":"Explain orphanRemoval as the JPA version of ON DELETE CASCADE but at the collection level, and warn about CascadeType.ALL on @ManyToMany."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"CascadeType.ALL on @ManyToMany Deletes Everything \u2014 Including Other Parents' Children",
         "content":"Cascade types propagate lifecycle operations from a parent entity to its children. `CascadeType.PERSIST` means persisting the parent also persists new children. `CascadeType.MERGE` means merging the parent also merges children. These are convenient and generally safe.\n\n**`CascadeType.REMOVE` and `CascadeType.ALL` are where the danger lives.** On a `@OneToMany` relationship, `CascadeType.REMOVE` makes sense \u2014 deleting an order deletes its line items. On a `@ManyToMany`, it's catastrophic. Deleting a student with `CascadeType.ALL` on `courses` deletes the courses, which may cascade to delete other students enrolled in those courses. **One delete can empty the entire database.**\n\n**`orphanRemoval = true`** is the collection-level delete trigger. It deletes child entities when they're removed from the parent's collection \u2014 `order.getItems().remove(item)` deletes the item from the database. Without `orphanRemoval`, removing from the collection only breaks the relationship; the orphaned row stays in the database."},
        {"type":"step","title":"cascade REMOVE vs orphanRemoval \u2014 The Subtle but Critical Difference",
         "content":"Both delete children, but at different trigger points.\n\n**`cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE`** fires when the parent is deleted. Deleting an `Order` deletes all its `OrderItems`. But removing an item from `order.getItems()` without deleting the order just nullifies the foreign key \u2014 the item row stays in the database as an orphan.\n\n**`orphanRemoval = true`** fires when a child is removed from the collection, regardless of whether the parent is deleted. `order.getItems().remove(item)` deletes the item from the database. It also implicitly includes `cascade = REMOVE` behavior \u2014 deleting the parent deletes all children.\n\nUse `orphanRemoval = true` when children have no meaning without the parent \u2014 order line items, phone numbers on a contact, addresses on a user. **Don't use it when children can exist independently** \u2014 tags on an article, categories on a product \u2014 because removing from the collection would delete the tag/category itself, affecting other entities that reference it.\n\nThe safe default for `@OneToMany` in a parent-child relationship: `@OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)`."},
    ],
    "Cascade types propagate operations from parent to child. `CascadeType.PERSIST` and `MERGE` are generally safe. **`CascadeType.REMOVE` and `ALL` are dangerous on @ManyToMany** \u2014 deleting one parent cascades to delete shared children, which can cascade to delete other parents.\n\nThe key distinction is **cascade REMOVE vs orphanRemoval.** Cascade REMOVE fires when the parent is deleted \u2014 delete the order, delete its items. `orphanRemoval = true` fires when a child is removed from the collection \u2014 `order.getItems().remove(item)` deletes the item from the database even though the order still exists.\n\nUse `orphanRemoval` when children can't exist without the parent \u2014 order line items, contact phone numbers. Don't use it for shared entities like tags or categories where removing from one collection shouldn't delete the entity.\n\nIn practice, for true parent-child `@OneToMany`, I use `cascade = ALL, orphanRemoval = true`. For `@ManyToMany`, I never use `CascadeType.REMOVE` or `ALL`.",
    "- **CascadeType.ALL on @ManyToMany can empty the database** \u2014 deleting one parent cascades to delete shared children, which cascades to their other parents; never use REMOVE or ALL on @ManyToMany.\n- **orphanRemoval deletes children when removed from the collection** \u2014 `order.getItems().remove(item)` fires a DELETE; without orphanRemoval, the item row stays in the database with a null foreign key.\n- **cascade REMOVE only fires when the parent is deleted** \u2014 removing a child from the collection without deleting the parent just breaks the relationship; orphanRemoval covers this gap.\n- **orphanRemoval implicitly includes cascade REMOVE** \u2014 setting `orphanRemoval = true` means deleting the parent also deletes all children; no need to add `CascadeType.REMOVE` separately.\n- **Don't use orphanRemoval on shared entities** \u2014 tags, categories, or courses that belong to multiple parents are deleted when removed from any one parent's collection; use only for exclusive parent-child relationships."
)

